            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-spheres"} n-spheres
            display various interesting patterns in their classification.
          %p This entry is meant to eventually list and discuss some of these. For the moment it mainly just collects some references.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#FreeActionsByFiniteGroupsAndSphericalSpaceForms Free actions by finite groups and spherical space forms
          %p
            We discuss some aspects of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-space"}> continuous
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+actions"} free actions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+groups"} finite groups
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-spheres"} n-spheres
          %p
            If the action is by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isometries"} isometries
            (with respect to the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/round+sphere"} round metric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quotient+spaces"} topological quotient spaces
            of such free actions are traditionally known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spherical+space+forms"} spherical space forms
            (going back to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "spherical+space+form#Killing1891"} Killing 1891
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#MadsenThomasWall76"} Madsen, Thomas &amp; Wall 1976
            %em topological spherical space forms
            for quotients by actions which are continuous but not necessarily by isometries.
